    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how lactic acid actually works in your muscles during high intensity training? like why does it make them burn and how does the body get rid of it after the workout is over?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lactic acid is a bit of a misnomer—it's actually lactate, and it's a byproduct of rapid energy production when oxygen runs low. During high-intensity training, your muscles switch to fast glycolysis, producing lactate and hydrogen ions (which cause the burning sensation) faster than they can be cleared. The burn is primarily from that acidity, not the lactate itself. After you stop, your body clears it via the Cori cycle, where the liver converts lactate back into glucose, and some is also used directly by the heart and other muscles as fuel. The process is quick—most lactate is gone within an hour, though soreness days later is a separate, inflammation-driven phenomenon.",
      "source": "target",
